  "client_view_text": " Use the information from \"Database System Concepts\" 4th edition by Abraham Silberschatz, Henry F. Korth and S. Sudarshan to support your answer.\nA write-ahead log (WAL) in a database system ensures that the database remains consistent and crash recovery is possible even after a system failure. The WAL is a record of all database operations that have been written to disk, prior to the actual update of the data itself. Each operation is recorded in the log in the order they were executed, and the log is typically implemented as a circular buffer, with the oldest records being overwritten to make space for new ones.\nWhen a crash occurs, the database system can recover the database to a consistent state by replaying all the operations recorded in the log. The process of recovery involves several steps: First, the database system identifies the last checkpoint, which is the most recent point in time when all data was consistent. The system then scans the log to find any transactions that were partially completed at the time of the crash. The partially completed transactions are then rolled back to ensure that the database is in a consistent state. Finally, the database system reapplies all transactions that were committed prior to the last checkpoint, to bring the database up to date.\nThe use"
